-- AKU science reasoning, batch 1: 60 original MCQs (BIO-8001..8020, CHE-8001..8020, PHY-8001..8020).
-- The AKU test's second section is Science and Mathematics Reasoning. The bank had the mathematics half (MAT-) but
-- no science reasoning. These follow the kinds of task in AKU's Science Reasoning practice set (aku.edu, admissions/
-- Documents/pr-mbbs-science-reasoning.pdf): reading a results table, fair tests and controls, circuits, gears and
-- linkages described in words, gas tests, electrolysis, gas laws, blood groups, enzymes. Written for Markhor Prep, not
-- copied from AKU or any book; every number was recomputed and every key rechecked.
--
-- Only in the AKU bank (questions.mdcat = 0), under the chapter "Science reasoning" of their subject. Not in the free
-- set. INSERT OR IGNORE keeps the file safe to run twice.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, mdcat, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, 'Science reasoning', s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-8001', 'Biology', 'Blood groups', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'ABO blood group is controlled by three alleles: Iᴬ, Iᴮ and i. Group A is IᴬIᴬ or Iᴬi, group B is IᴮIᴮ or Iᴮi, group AB is IᴬIᴮ and group O is ii.
One parent is group AB and the other is group O. Which blood groups can their children have?', '["AB or O only", "A, B, AB or O", "A or B only", "AB only"]', 2, 'The AB parent passes on Iᴬ or Iᴮ and the O parent always passes on i. Every child is Iᴬi (group A) or Iᴮi (group B). No child can be AB or O.', '{"0": "A child gets only one allele from each parent, so it cannot copy either parent''s group here.", "1": "AB needs Iᴬ and Iᴮ together and O needs two i alleles; neither is possible.", "3": "The group O parent has no Iᴬ or Iᴮ allele to give."}'),
  ('BIO-8002', 'Biology', 'Blood groups', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'ABO blood group is controlled by three alleles: Iᴬ, Iᴮ and i. Group A is IᴬIᴬ or Iᴬi, group B is IᴮIᴮ or Iᴮi, group AB is IᴬIᴮ and group O is ii.
A father with genotype Iᴬi and a mother with genotype Iᴮi have a child. What is the chance that the child is group O?', '["1 in 2", "1 in 4", "0", "1 in 16"]', 1, 'Group O needs i from both parents. Each parent passes on i half the time, so the chance is ½ × ½ = ¼. The four equally likely children are IᴬIᴮ, Iᴬi, Iᴮi and ii.', '{"0": "That is the chance of getting i from one parent only.", "2": "Both parents carry the i allele, so an ii child is possible.", "3": "That multiplies ¼ by ¼."}'),
  ('BIO-8003', 'Biology', 'Blood transfusion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Red blood cells of group A carry antigen A, group B carry antigen B, group AB carry both and group O carry neither. Plasma of group A contains anti-B antibodies, group B contains anti-A, group AB contains neither and group O contains both. Donated red cells are destroyed if the patient''s plasma contains an antibody against them.
A patient is group B. Red cells from which donors are safe?', '["Group A and group AB", "Group B and group O", "Group AB only", "Any group"]', 1, 'The patient''s plasma has anti-A antibodies, which attack any cell carrying antigen A. Cells of group B (antigen B only) and group O (no antigens) are not attacked. Group A and AB cells carry antigen A and would be destroyed.', '{"0": "Both of these carry antigen A, which the patient''s anti-A antibodies attack.", "2": "AB cells carry antigen A.", "3": "Cells with antigen A would be destroyed."}'),
  ('BIO-8004', 'Biology', 'Enzymes and pH', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'The activity of a digestive enzyme is measured at five pH values.
pH 2: 5 units
pH 4: 20 units
pH 6: 60 units
pH 8: 95 units
pH 10: 40 units
The stomach is at about pH 2 and the small intestine at about pH 8. Which conclusion is supported?', '["The enzyme works best at pH 2", "The enzyme would work much better in the small intestine than in the stomach", "The enzyme''s activity does not depend on pH", "The enzyme works equally well in the stomach and the small intestine"]', 1, 'Activity is highest near pH 8 (95 units) and very low at pH 2 (5 units). The enzyme suits the conditions of the small intestine, not the stomach.', '{"0": "pH 2 gives the lowest activity in the table.", "2": "Activity changes from 5 to 95 units across the pH range.", "3": "It is 19 times more active at pH 8 than at pH 2."}'),
  ('BIO-8005', 'Biology', 'Enzymes and temperature', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Amylase is mixed with starch in four tubes kept at different temperatures. A drop from each is tested with iodine every minute until the blue-black colour no longer appears.
20 °C: 12 min
37 °C: 4 min
60 °C: 9 min
90 °C: still blue-black after 30 min
The 90 °C tube is then cooled to 37 °C and tested for another 30 min. What result is expected?', '["The starch is digested in about 4 min", "The starch is still not digested", "The starch is digested in about 12 min", "The starch is digested in less than 4 min"]', 1, 'At 90 °C the enzyme is denatured: its active site loses its shape. This change is permanent, so cooling does not restore the enzyme and the starch stays undigested. Slow activity at 20 °C is different, because cold only slows the molecules down.', '{"0": "That would need working enzyme, but heating destroyed its shape.", "2": "The enzyme is denatured, not just slowed.", "3": "Heating does not make the enzyme work faster afterwards."}'),
  ('BIO-8006', 'Biology', 'Variables', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Amylase is mixed with starch in four tubes kept at 20 °C, 37 °C, 60 °C and 90 °C. The time taken for the starch to disappear is recorded for each tube.
What is the independent variable, the one the experimenter chooses to change?', '["The time for the starch to disappear", "The volume of amylase", "The temperature", "The concentration of starch"]', 2, 'The experimenter sets a different temperature for each tube, so temperature is the independent variable. The time taken is what is measured (the dependent variable), and the amounts of amylase and starch are kept the same.', '{"0": "That is the result being measured, the dependent variable.", "1": "That should be kept the same in every tube.", "3": "That should be kept the same in every tube."}'),
  ('BIO-8007', 'Biology', 'Osmosis', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Potato cylinders are weighed, left in sucrose solutions for two hours and weighed again.
0.0 mol/dm³: mass up 12%
0.2 mol/dm³: mass up 5%
0.4 mol/dm³: mass down 2%
0.6 mol/dm³: mass down 9%
0.8 mol/dm³: mass down 14%
Which sucrose concentration has the same water potential as the potato cells?', '["0.0 mol/dm³", "Between 0.4 and 0.6 mol/dm³", "Above 0.8 mol/dm³", "Between 0.2 and 0.4 mol/dm³"]', 3, 'When the solution matches the cells there is no net movement of water and no change in mass. The change switches from a gain (+5%) at 0.2 to a loss (−2%) at 0.4, so the matching concentration lies between them, closer to 0.4.', '{"0": "The cylinder gained the most mass here, so water was entering.", "1": "The cylinders are already losing mass across this range.", "2": "The largest loss of mass is at 0.8, far from balance."}'),
  ('BIO-8008', 'Biology', 'Osmosis', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A potato cylinder left in pure water for two hours gains 12% in mass.
What is the best explanation?', '["Sucrose moved into the cells from the water", "The cells pumped water in by active transport using energy", "Water moved into the cells by osmosis because the water potential outside was higher", "The cell walls dissolved and absorbed water"]', 2, 'Pure water has the highest possible water potential, higher than the cell sap with its dissolved substances. Water moves through the partially permeable membranes into the cells, so the cylinder gets heavier and firmer.', '{"0": "There is no sucrose in pure water.", "1": "Water crosses membranes passively; cells do not pump it.", "3": "Cellulose walls do not dissolve in water."}'),
  ('BIO-8009', 'Biology', 'Family patterns', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A genetic condition appears in a girl. Neither of her parents has the condition.
Which explanation fits these facts?', '["The condition is dominant and both parents are homozygous for the normal allele", "The condition is recessive and both parents are heterozygous carriers", "The condition is recessive and only the mother carries the allele", "The condition is recessive and carried on the X chromosome, and the father has the normal allele"]', 1, 'Unaffected parents with an affected child means the allele is hidden in the parents, so it is recessive. The girl needs one copy from each parent, so both must be carriers. It cannot be X-linked: she would need an affected X from her father, and a father with that X would have the condition himself.', '{"0": "With no copy of the allele in either parent, the child could not inherit it.", "2": "She needs a copy of the recessive allele from each parent.", "3": "An affected daughter needs an affected X from her father, who would then show the condition."}'),
  ('BIO-8010', 'Biology', 'Sex linkage', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Red-green colour blindness is caused by a recessive allele on the X chromosome. A colour-blind man has children with a woman who has two normal alleles.
Which statement about their children is correct?', '["All the daughters are carriers and none of the sons is colour blind", "All the sons are colour blind", "Half the sons are colour blind", "Half the daughters are colour blind"]', 0, 'Sons get their X from the mother, and both of hers are normal, so no son is colour blind. Every daughter gets the father''s only X, which carries the allele, plus a normal X from the mother, so every daughter is a carrier with normal vision.', '{"1": "Sons receive the father''s Y chromosome, not his X.", "2": "Both of the mother''s X chromosomes are normal.", "3": "Each daughter has one normal X from her mother, which is enough for normal vision."}'),
  ('BIO-8011', 'Biology', 'Energy flow', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'In a food chain, only about 10% of the energy at one feeding level is passed on to the next. The producers in a field hold 50 000 kJ. The chain is: producers, primary consumers, secondary consumers, tertiary consumers.
About how much energy reaches the tertiary consumers?', '["500 kJ", "5000 kJ", "5 kJ", "50 kJ"]', 3, 'Three transfers separate the producers from the tertiary consumers, each passing on a tenth: 50 000 → 5000 → 500 → 50 kJ.', '{"0": "That is the energy at the secondary consumers.", "1": "That is the energy at the primary consumers.", "2": "That applies four transfers instead of three."}'),
  ('BIO-8012', 'Biology', 'Population growth', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A culture of bacteria doubles in number every 20 minutes while food is plentiful. It starts with 500 cells.
How many cells are there after 2 hours?', '["32 000", "3000", "16 000", "64 000"]', 0, 'Two hours is 120 minutes, which is 6 doublings. 500 × 2⁶ = 500 × 64 = 32 000 cells.', '{"1": "That multiplies by 6 instead of doubling 6 times.", "2": "That is 5 doublings, the number after 100 minutes.", "3": "That is 7 doublings, the number after 140 minutes."}'),
  ('BIO-8013', 'Biology', 'Limiting factors', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Pondweed is lit by a lamp and the bubbles of oxygen it gives off each minute are counted. The water temperature is kept constant.
Lamp at 40 cm: 10 bubbles
Lamp at 20 cm: 40 bubbles
Lamp at 10 cm: 80 bubbles
Lamp at 5 cm: 80 bubbles
What best explains the result at 5 cm?', '["The bright light has destroyed the chlorophyll", "The lamp has heated the plant and killed it", "Light is no longer the limiting factor, so something else such as carbon dioxide is holding the rate back", "The plant has stopped respiring"]', 2, 'Moving the lamp closer raised the rate up to 10 cm. Between 10 cm and 5 cm the light is stronger but the rate stays at 80, so light is no longer what limits photosynthesis. Another factor, such as the carbon dioxide supply, now sets the rate.', '{"0": "If chlorophyll were destroyed the rate would fall, not stay at 80.", "1": "The temperature is constant, and a dead plant would give no bubbles.", "3": "The bubbles come from photosynthesis, and they have not stopped."}'),
  ('BIO-8014', 'Biology', 'Measuring respiration', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Germinating seeds are sealed in a tube with a chemical that absorbs carbon dioxide. As the seeds use oxygen, a drop of liquid moves along a narrow glass tube towards them. The glass tube has a cross-sectional area of 1 mm², and the drop moves 24 mm in 6 minutes.
What is the rate of oxygen uptake?', '["24 mm³ per minute", "144 mm³ per minute", "4 mm³ per minute", "0.25 mm³ per minute"]', 2, 'The volume of oxygen used is the volume the drop sweeps out: 24 mm × 1 mm² = 24 mm³. That took 6 minutes, so the rate is 24 ÷ 6 = 4 mm³ per minute.', '{"0": "That is the total volume used in 6 minutes, not the rate per minute.", "1": "That multiplies by the time instead of dividing by it.", "3": "That divides the time by the distance."}'),
  ('BIO-8015', 'Biology', 'Heart output', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Cardiac output is the volume of blood the heart pumps each minute: heart rate × the volume pumped per beat. At rest a student''s heart beats 75 times a minute and pumps 80 cm³ per beat. During exercise it beats 150 times a minute and pumps 100 cm³ per beat.
How many times greater is the cardiac output during exercise than at rest?', '["2.5 times", "2.0 times", "1.25 times", "9 times"]', 0, 'At rest: 75 × 80 = 6000 cm³ per minute. During exercise: 150 × 100 = 15 000 cm³ per minute. 15 000 ÷ 6000 = 2.5.', '{"1": "That compares only the heart rates.", "2": "That compares only the volumes per beat.", "3": "9 dm³ is the difference between the two outputs, not how many times greater."}'),
  ('BIO-8016', 'Biology', 'Using a key', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A key for sorting animals reads:
1. Has a backbone: go to 2. No backbone: invertebrate.
2. Has feathers: bird. No feathers: go to 3.
3. Has hair or fur: mammal. No hair or fur: go to 4.
4. Dry skin with scales: reptile. Moist skin without scales: amphibian.
An animal has a backbone, no feathers, no hair and moist skin without scales. What is it?', '["A reptile", "A mammal", "An amphibian", "An invertebrate"]', 2, 'Backbone leads to step 2, no feathers to step 3, no hair to step 4, and moist skin without scales gives amphibian.', '{"0": "Reptiles have dry, scaly skin.", "1": "Mammals have hair or fur.", "3": "Invertebrates have no backbone."}'),
  ('BIO-8017', 'Biology', 'Controls', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Bacteria are spread over a jelly plate. Three paper discs are placed on it: one soaked in antibiotic A, one in antibiotic B and one in sterile water. After a day the plate is checked for clear zones where bacteria have not grown.
What is the purpose of the disc soaked in sterile water?', '["To keep the jelly moist so the bacteria can grow", "To dilute the antibiotics on the other discs", "To show that the paper and water alone do not stop the bacteria growing", "To kill any unwanted microbes on the plate"]', 2, 'The water disc is a control. If bacteria grow right up to it, any clear zone round the other discs must be caused by the antibiotic and not by the paper or the liquid.', '{"0": "The jelly already contains the water the bacteria need.", "1": "It does not touch the other discs.", "3": "Sterile water does not kill microbes."}'),
  ('BIO-8018', 'Biology', 'Interpreting results', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Discs soaked in equal doses of three antibiotics are placed on a plate spread with one type of bacterium. After a day the width of the clear zone round each disc is measured.
Antibiotic A: 4 mm
Antibiotic B: 18 mm
Antibiotic C: 0 mm
Which conclusion is supported?', '["C kills this bacterium the fastest", "At this dose B is the most effective against this bacterium, and C has no effect on it", "A must be more concentrated than B", "B will cure any bacterial infection"]', 1, 'A wider clear zone means the antibiotic stopped growth further from the disc. B has the widest zone, and C has none, so this bacterium is unaffected by C. The results say nothing about other bacteria.', '{"0": "No clear zone means the bacteria grew right up to the disc.", "2": "The doses were equal, and A''s zone is smaller.", "3": "Only one type of bacterium was tested."}'),
  ('BIO-8019', 'Biology', 'The cell cycle', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'In a growing root tip the time a cell spends in each stage is in proportion to the number of cells seen in that stage. Out of 200 cells: 160 are in interphase, 20 in prophase, 8 in metaphase, 4 in anaphase and 8 in telophase. One full cell cycle takes 20 hours.
About how long does metaphase last?', '["8 minutes", "2 hours", "48 minutes", "16 hours"]', 2, 'Metaphase is 8 of 200 cells, which is 4% of the cycle. 4% of 20 hours is 0.8 hours, or 48 minutes.', '{"0": "That reads the cell count as minutes.", "1": "That is the time for prophase (20 of 200 cells).", "3": "That is the time for interphase (160 of 200 cells)."}'),
  ('BIO-8020', 'Biology', 'Test crosses', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'In pea plants the allele for tall stems (T) is dominant to the allele for short stems (t). A tall plant is crossed with a short plant. The seeds grow into 52 tall plants and 48 short plants.
What is the genotype of the tall parent?', '["TT", "tt", "It cannot be worked out from these results", "Tt"]', 3, 'Short offspring are tt, so each got a t from the tall parent, which must therefore carry t. A Tt × tt cross gives about half tall and half short, matching 52 : 48.', '{"0": "A TT parent would give only tall offspring.", "1": "A tt plant would be short, not tall.", "2": "The roughly 1 : 1 ratio shows the tall parent is heterozygous."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, mdcat, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, 'Science reasoning', s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-8001', 'Chemistry', 'Identifying gases', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A white solid is heated in a test tube. The colourless gas given off has no smell, has no effect on damp litmus paper, and makes a glowing splint burst into flame.
Which gas is it?', '["Hydrogen", "Carbon dioxide", "Ammonia", "Oxygen"]', 3, 'Relighting a glowing splint is the test for oxygen, which supports burning. Oxygen is neutral, so damp litmus is unchanged.', '{"0": "Hydrogen burns with a squeaky pop at a lighted splint; it does not relight a glowing one.", "1": "Carbon dioxide puts a splint out and turns damp blue litmus red.", "2": "Ammonia has a sharp smell and turns damp red litmus blue."}'),
  ('CHE-8002', 'Chemistry', 'Identifying gases', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A colourless gas turns limewater milky. When it is bubbled through acidified potassium dichromate solution, the solution stays orange.
Which gas is it most likely to be?', '["Sulfur dioxide", "Oxygen", "Carbon dioxide", "Nitrogen"]', 2, 'Both carbon dioxide and sulfur dioxide give a white precipitate with limewater. Sulfur dioxide is a reducing agent and would turn acidified dichromate from orange to green. No colour change rules it out and leaves carbon dioxide.', '{"0": "Sulfur dioxide would turn the orange dichromate green.", "1": "Oxygen does not turn limewater milky.", "3": "Nitrogen does not react with limewater."}'),
  ('CHE-8003', 'Chemistry', 'Electrolysis', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Solid lead(II) bromide does not conduct electricity. It is melted, and a current is passed through the liquid between two carbon rods.
What forms at the negative rod (the cathode)?', '["Lead", "Bromine", "Hydrogen", "Oxygen"]', 0, 'Molten lead(II) bromide contains only Pb²⁺ and Br⁻ ions, now free to move. Positive lead ions travel to the negative rod and gain electrons, forming lead metal. Bromide ions lose electrons at the positive rod and form bromine.', '{"1": "Bromide ions are negative and are discharged at the positive rod.", "2": "No water is present in the molten salt, so there is no hydrogen to release.", "3": "The melt contains no oxygen."}'),
  ('CHE-8004', 'Chemistry', 'Electrolysis', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Concentrated sodium chloride solution is electrolysed with carbon rods. Hydrogen bubbles off at the negative rod and chlorine at the positive rod.
Which substance builds up in the solution that is left?', '["Hydrochloric acid", "Sodium metal", "Pure water", "Sodium hydroxide"]', 3, 'The solution holds Na⁺, Cl⁻, H⁺ and OH⁻ ions. H⁺ and Cl⁻ are removed as hydrogen and chlorine. The Na⁺ and OH⁻ ions stay behind, so the solution becomes sodium hydroxide.', '{"0": "Its ions, H⁺ and Cl⁻, are exactly the ones being removed.", "1": "Sodium is too reactive to form in water; hydrogen is released instead.", "2": "The sodium and hydroxide ions remain dissolved."}'),
  ('CHE-8005', 'Chemistry', 'Molar gas volume', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'One mole of any gas occupies 22.4 dm³ at standard temperature and pressure (STP). The molar mass of carbon dioxide is 44 g/mol.
What is the mass of 5.6 dm³ of carbon dioxide at STP?', '["44 g", "22 g", "11 g", "176 g"]', 2, '5.6 dm³ is 5.6 ÷ 22.4 = 0.25 mol. The mass is 0.25 × 44 = 11 g.', '{"0": "That is the mass of a whole mole, 22.4 dm³.", "1": "That is the mass of half a mole, 11.2 dm³.", "3": "That multiplies by 4 instead of dividing by 4."}'),
  ('CHE-8006', 'Chemistry', 'Gas pressure and volume', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A fixed mass of gas in a syringe has a volume of 2.0 dm³ at a pressure of 100 kPa. The plunger is pushed in slowly so that the temperature stays the same, until the volume is 0.5 dm³.
What is the new pressure?', '["400 kPa", "25 kPa", "200 kPa", "50 kPa"]', 0, 'At constant temperature, pressure × volume stays constant: 100 × 2.0 = 200. At 0.5 dm³ the pressure is 200 ÷ 0.5 = 400 kPa. A quarter of the volume means four times the pressure.', '{"1": "Squeezing a gas raises its pressure; this divides by 4 instead of multiplying.", "2": "The volume fell to a quarter, not a half.", "3": "Pressure rises when volume falls."}'),
  ('CHE-8007', 'Chemistry', 'Gas volume and temperature', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A gas has a volume of 300 cm³ at 27 °C. It is heated to 127 °C at constant pressure.
What is its new volume?', '["1411 cm³", "225 cm³", "400 cm³", "427 cm³"]', 2, 'Volume is proportional to the kelvin temperature. 27 °C = 300 K and 127 °C = 400 K, so the volume becomes 300 × 400 ÷ 300 = 400 cm³.', '{"0": "That uses the Celsius values (300 × 127 ÷ 27) instead of kelvin.", "1": "Heating at constant pressure makes a gas expand, not shrink.", "3": "That adds 127 to the volume."}'),
  ('CHE-8008', 'Chemistry', 'Rates of reaction', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Equal lengths of magnesium ribbon are dropped into equal volumes of hydrochloric acid at the same temperature. The time for the ribbon to disappear is measured.
0.5 mol/dm³ acid: 120 s
1.0 mol/dm³ acid: 60 s
2.0 mol/dm³ acid: 30 s
Which conclusion do the results support?', '["The rate of reaction is directly proportional to the acid concentration", "The time taken is directly proportional to the acid concentration", "The rate of reaction does not depend on the acid concentration", "The rate of reaction is proportional to the square of the acid concentration"]', 0, 'Each time the concentration doubles, the time halves, so the same amount of magnesium reacts twice as fast. Rate is proportional to 1 ÷ time, so rate is directly proportional to concentration.', '{"1": "The time falls as concentration rises; it is inversely proportional.", "2": "The times change from 120 s to 30 s.", "3": "Doubling the concentration doubles the rate; it does not make it four times as fast."}'),
  ('CHE-8009', 'Chemistry', 'Fair tests', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A student compares how fast magnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid of three different concentrations.
Which of these would make the comparison unfair?', '["Using magnesium powder in one run and magnesium ribbon in the others", "Using the same volume of acid in every run", "Starting every run at the same temperature", "Timing every run until the magnesium has just disappeared"]', 0, 'Powder has a much larger surface area than ribbon and reacts faster. A faster run could then be due to the powder, not the acid concentration. Only the concentration should differ between runs.', '{"1": "Keeping the volume the same helps make the test fair.", "2": "Keeping the temperature the same helps make the test fair.", "3": "Using one end point for every run keeps the timing consistent."}'),
  ('CHE-8010', 'Chemistry', 'Titration', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', '25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution is exactly neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid. The two react in a 1 : 1 ratio.
What is the concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution?', '["0.125 mol/dm³", "0.100 mol/dm³", "0.080 mol/dm³", "0.0020 mol/dm³"]', 2, 'Moles of acid = 0.100 × 20.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.0020 mol, so 0.0020 mol of sodium hydroxide reacted. Its concentration is 0.0020 ÷ 0.0250 dm³ = 0.080 mol/dm³. It is weaker than the acid because more of it was needed.', '{"0": "That divides the volumes the wrong way round (25.0 ÷ 20.0).", "1": "The volumes are different, so the concentrations cannot be equal.", "3": "That is the number of moles that reacted, not the concentration."}'),
  ('CHE-8011', 'Chemistry', 'Energy changes', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Equal masses of three solids are each stirred into 50 cm³ of water at 20 °C, and the final temperature is recorded.
Solid A: 26 °C
Solid B: 16 °C
Solid C: 20 °C
Which solid could be used in an instant cold pack?', '["Solid A", "Solid C", "Solid B", "Either A or B"]', 2, 'A cold pack needs a change that takes in heat from its surroundings (endothermic), which makes the temperature fall. Only solid B lowers the temperature, from 20 °C to 16 °C.', '{"0": "A raises the temperature, so dissolving it gives out heat.", "1": "C causes no temperature change.", "3": "A warms the water; only B cools it."}'),
  ('CHE-8012', 'Chemistry', 'Solubility', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'The solubility of potassium nitrate is 110 g per 100 g of water at 60 °C and 32 g per 100 g of water at 20 °C.
A saturated solution containing 50 g of water at 60 °C is cooled to 20 °C. What mass of crystals forms?', '["78 g", "39 g", "55 g", "16 g"]', 1, 'In 50 g of water the solution holds 110 ÷ 2 = 55 g at 60 °C but can hold only 32 ÷ 2 = 16 g at 20 °C. The difference, 55 − 16 = 39 g, comes out as crystals.', '{"0": "That is the answer for 100 g of water, not 50 g.", "2": "That is the mass dissolved at the start, not the mass that crystallises.", "3": "That is the mass that stays dissolved at 20 °C."}'),
  ('CHE-8013', 'Chemistry', 'Chromatography', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'In paper chromatography the Rf value of a dye is the distance moved by the dye divided by the distance moved by the solvent. In one solvent, dye P has Rf 0.25, dye Q has Rf 0.40 and dye R has Rf 0.80.
An unknown spot moves 4.0 cm while the solvent moves 10.0 cm. Which dye is it?', '["Dye Q", "Dye P", "Dye R", "None of the three"]', 0, 'Rf = 4.0 ÷ 10.0 = 0.40, which matches dye Q in this solvent.', '{"1": "P would have moved only 2.5 cm.", "2": "R would have moved 8.0 cm.", "3": "0.40 matches the value given for Q."}'),
  ('CHE-8014', 'Chemistry', 'Crystal structures', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'In sodium chloride, NaCl, each sodium ion is surrounded by 6 chloride ions and each chloride ion by 6 sodium ions. The two numbers are equal because the ions are present in a 1 : 1 ratio.
In a crystal of formula XY₂, each X ion is surrounded by 8 Y ions. How many X ions surround each Y ion?', '["8", "16", "2", "4"]', 3, 'Count the X–Y contacts from both sides. Each X touches 8 Y ions, and there are twice as many Y ions as X ions, so the same contacts are shared among twice as many ions: 8 ÷ 2 = 4 X ions around each Y.', '{"0": "The numbers are equal only when the ions are in a 1 : 1 ratio.", "1": "There are more Y ions, so each has fewer X neighbours, not more.", "2": "That halves the number twice."}'),
  ('CHE-8015', 'Chemistry', 'Reactivity series', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Four metals, A, B, C and D, are tested with solutions of each other''s salts. A displaces B and C from their salts. C displaces B. D displaces A.
What is the order of reactivity, from most to least reactive?', '["B, C, A, D", "D, A, C, B", "D, A, B, C", "A, D, C, B"]', 1, 'A more reactive metal displaces a less reactive one. D displaces A, so D is above A. A displaces C and B, and C displaces B, so the order is D, A, C, B.', '{"0": "That is the order from least to most reactive.", "2": "C displaces B, so C must be above B.", "3": "D displaces A, so D must be above A."}'),
  ('CHE-8016', 'Chemistry', 'Indicators', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Indicator X is red below pH 4 and yellow above pH 6. Indicator Y is colourless below pH 8 and pink above pH 10.
A solution is yellow with indicator X and colourless with indicator Y. What can be said about its pH?', '["It is below 4", "It is between 6 and 8", "It is between 4 and 6", "It is above 10"]', 1, 'Yellow with X means the pH is above 6. Colourless with Y means it is below 8. Both are true only between pH 6 and pH 8.', '{"0": "X would be red below pH 4.", "2": "X would be between red and yellow there, not fully yellow.", "3": "Y would be pink above pH 10."}'),
  ('CHE-8017', 'Chemistry', 'Empirical formulae', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A sample of a compound contains 2.4 g of carbon, 0.4 g of hydrogen and 3.2 g of oxygen. (Relative atomic masses: C = 12, H = 1, O = 16.)
What is its empirical formula?', '["CH₂O", "CH₄O", "C₂H₄O", "CHO₂"]', 0, 'Moles: carbon 2.4 ÷ 12 = 0.2, hydrogen 0.4 ÷ 1 = 0.4, oxygen 3.2 ÷ 16 = 0.2. Dividing by the smallest gives 1 : 2 : 1, so the empirical formula is CH₂O.', '{"1": "That would need four hydrogen atoms for each carbon; the ratio is two.", "2": "Carbon and oxygen are present in equal numbers of moles.", "3": "Carbon and oxygen are present in equal numbers of moles, not 1 : 2."}'),
  ('CHE-8018', 'Chemistry', 'Reacting gas volumes', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Hydrogen and oxygen react in the ratio 2 volumes of hydrogen to 1 volume of oxygen, forming water. 10 cm³ of hydrogen is mixed with 10 cm³ of oxygen and sparked. The mixture is cooled to room temperature, so the water formed is liquid.
What gas is left?', '["10 cm³ of oxygen", "5 cm³ of oxygen", "5 cm³ of hydrogen", "No gas is left"]', 1, '10 cm³ of hydrogen needs only 5 cm³ of oxygen. All the hydrogen is used up and 10 − 5 = 5 cm³ of oxygen remains. The water condenses and takes up almost no volume.', '{"0": "Half of the oxygen is used up by the hydrogen.", "2": "Hydrogen is the gas that runs out.", "3": "There is twice as much oxygen as the hydrogen needs."}'),
  ('CHE-8019', 'Chemistry', 'Equilibrium', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A sealed tube contains two gases in equilibrium: colourless N₂O₄ and brown NO₂. Turning N₂O₄ into NO₂ takes in heat.
The tube is placed in ice. What is seen?', '["The brown colour becomes darker", "Nothing changes, because the tube is sealed", "The gas turns from brown to green", "The brown colour becomes paler"]', 3, 'Cooling removes heat, and the equilibrium shifts in the direction that gives out heat. That is the reverse change, NO₂ into colourless N₂O₄, so there is less brown gas and the colour fades.', '{"0": "That would happen on heating, which favours the heat-absorbing change to NO₂.", "1": "A sealed tube keeps the gases in, but temperature still shifts the equilibrium.", "2": "Neither gas is green."}'),
  ('CHE-8020', 'Chemistry', 'Half-life', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'The concentration of a reactant is measured as it breaks down.
0 min: 0.80 mol/dm³
10 min: 0.40 mol/dm³
20 min: 0.20 mol/dm³
If the pattern continues, what will the concentration be at 40 min?', '["0.05 mol/dm³", "0.10 mol/dm³", "0.00 mol/dm³", "0.025 mol/dm³"]', 0, 'The concentration halves every 10 minutes. At 30 min it is 0.10 and at 40 min it is 0.05 mol/dm³. Four halvings give 0.80 ÷ 16 = 0.05.', '{"1": "That is the value at 30 min.", "2": "Halving never reaches zero; a straight-line fall would be the wrong pattern.", "3": "That is the value at 50 min."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, mdcat, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, 'Science reasoning', s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-8001', 'Physics', 'Series circuits', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A 12 V battery is connected to three resistors in series: 2 Ω, 4 Ω and 6 Ω. The battery and wires have no resistance.
What is the voltage across the 4 Ω resistor?', '["12 V", "4 V", "2 V", "6 V"]', 1, 'In series the resistances add: 2 + 4 + 6 = 12 Ω, so the current is 12 V ÷ 12 Ω = 1 A everywhere. Across the 4 Ω resistor V = IR = 1 × 4 = 4 V. The three voltages (2 V, 4 V, 6 V) add up to the battery''s 12 V.', '{"0": "That is the battery voltage, which is shared between all three resistors.", "2": "That is the voltage across the 2 Ω resistor.", "3": "That is the voltage across the 6 Ω resistor."}'),
  ('PHY-8002', 'Physics', 'Bulbs in series and parallel', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Three identical bulbs are connected to a battery. Bulb A is in the main line. Bulbs B and C are side by side in parallel with each other, and this pair is in series with A.
Bulb C burns out, leaving a gap in its branch. What happens to bulbs A and B?', '["A and B both become brighter", "A becomes dimmer and B becomes brighter", "A and B both go out", "A becomes brighter and B becomes dimmer"]', 1, 'Call each bulb''s resistance R. Before, the pair B and C is worth R/2, the total is 1.5R and A carries V/1.5R = 0.67 V/R, while B carries half of that, 0.33 V/R. After C fails, A and B are simply in series: total 2R, and both carry 0.5 V/R. A''s current falls (dimmer) and B''s rises (brighter).', '{"0": "Losing a parallel branch raises the total resistance, so the main current through A falls.", "2": "B''s branch is still complete, so current still flows through A and B.", "3": "This has the two changes the wrong way round."}'),
  ('PHY-8003', 'Physics', 'Parallel circuits', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A 6 V battery is connected across a 3 Ω resistor and a 6 Ω resistor that are in parallel with each other. An ammeter is placed in the main line next to the battery.
What does the ammeter read?', '["0.67 A", "3 A", "2 A", "1 A"]', 1, 'Each parallel branch has the full 6 V across it. The 3 Ω branch carries 6 ÷ 3 = 2 A and the 6 Ω branch carries 6 ÷ 6 = 1 A. The main line carries both: 2 + 1 = 3 A.', '{"0": "That treats the resistors as if they were in series (6 V ÷ 9 Ω).", "2": "That is the current in the 3 Ω branch only.", "3": "That is the current in the 6 Ω branch only."}'),
  ('PHY-8004', 'Physics', 'Gears', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Gear A has 20 teeth and meshes directly with gear B, which has 50 teeth. Gear A turns clockwise at 150 revolutions per minute.
How does gear B turn?', '["Anticlockwise at 60 revolutions per minute", "Anticlockwise at 375 revolutions per minute", "Clockwise at 60 revolutions per minute", "Clockwise at 375 revolutions per minute"]', 0, 'Two meshed gears turn in opposite directions, so B turns anticlockwise. The same number of teeth pass the contact point each minute: 20 × 150 = 3000 teeth, and 3000 ÷ 50 = 60 revolutions. The larger gear turns more slowly.', '{"1": "The bigger gear turns more slowly, not faster.", "2": "Meshed gears turn in opposite directions.", "3": "Wrong direction, and the bigger gear turns more slowly."}'),
  ('PHY-8005', 'Physics', 'Gears', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Three gears are meshed in a line. Gear A (12 teeth) drives gear B (36 teeth), and gear B drives gear C (24 teeth). Gear A turns clockwise at 100 revolutions per minute.
How does gear C turn?', '["Anticlockwise at 50 revolutions per minute", "Clockwise at 33 revolutions per minute", "Clockwise at 200 revolutions per minute", "Clockwise at 50 revolutions per minute"]', 3, 'Each mesh reverses the direction: A clockwise, B anticlockwise, C clockwise. The same number of teeth pass every contact point, 12 × 100 = 1200 a minute, so C makes 1200 ÷ 24 = 50 revolutions. The middle gear changes the direction but not the overall ratio.', '{"0": "Two meshes reverse the direction twice, so C turns the same way as A.", "1": "That is the speed of the middle gear B (1200 ÷ 36).", "2": "C has more teeth than A, so it turns more slowly than A."}'),
  ('PHY-8006', 'Physics', 'Moments', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A uniform metre rule is balanced on a pivot at its centre. A 4 N weight hangs 30 cm to the left of the pivot.
How far to the right of the pivot must a 6 N weight hang to keep the rule balanced?', '["20 cm", "45 cm", "30 cm", "12 cm"]', 0, 'For balance the turning effects are equal: 4 N × 30 cm = 120 N cm on the left, so 6 N × d = 120 and d = 20 cm. The heavier weight sits closer to the pivot.', '{"1": "That multiplies by 6/4 instead of dividing; the heavier weight must be nearer the pivot.", "2": "Equal distances only balance equal weights.", "3": "This does not give equal moments: 6 × 12 = 72, not 120."}'),
  ('PHY-8007', 'Physics', 'Pulleys', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A load of 200 N hangs from a single movable pulley. One end of the rope is tied to the ceiling and a worker pulls the other end straight up, so two sections of rope support the pulley. Ignore friction and the weight of the pulley.
The worker raises the load by 1.5 m. What force is used, and how much rope is pulled?', '["200 N, pulling 1.5 m of rope", "100 N, pulling 1.5 m of rope", "400 N, pulling 0.75 m of rope", "100 N, pulling 3.0 m of rope"]', 3, 'Two rope sections share the load, so each carries 200 ÷ 2 = 100 N. To lift the load 1.5 m both sections must shorten by 1.5 m, so 3.0 m of rope is pulled. The work matches: 100 N × 3.0 m = 200 N × 1.5 m = 300 J.', '{"0": "That is what a single fixed pulley would need.", "1": "Half the force must be paid for with twice the distance.", "2": "A movable pulley reduces the force, it does not double it."}'),
  ('PHY-8008', 'Physics', 'Scale drawing tools', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A drawing tool has a fixed pivot, a tracing pin and a pencil. Its arms keep the pencil on the straight line from the pivot through the pin, always 2.5 times as far from the pivot as the pin is.
The pin is moved round a square with sides of 4 cm. What is the area of the square drawn by the pencil?', '["40 cm²", "10 cm²", "100 cm²", "64 cm²"]', 2, 'Every length is enlarged 2.5 times, so the drawn square has sides of 4 × 2.5 = 10 cm and an area of 10 × 10 = 100 cm². Area grows by the square of the scale factor: 16 × 2.5² = 100.', '{"0": "That multiplies the area by 2.5 once; area scales by 2.5 squared.", "1": "That is the length of one side of the new square, not its area.", "3": "That doubles each side instead of multiplying by 2.5."}'),
  ('PHY-8009', 'Physics', 'Linkages', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Two straight rods, 9 cm and 5 cm long, are hinged together at one end so the angle between them can be anything from fully folded to fully straight. The free end of one rod is at point P and the free end of the other is at point Q.
What are the largest and smallest possible distances between P and Q?', '["14 cm and 0 cm", "9 cm and 5 cm", "14 cm and 4 cm", "45 cm and 4 cm"]', 2, 'With the rods in one straight line the ends are 9 + 5 = 14 cm apart. Folded back on each other, the shorter rod lies along the longer one and the ends are 9 − 5 = 4 cm apart. Every other angle gives a distance in between.', '{"0": "The ends could only meet if the rods were the same length.", "1": "Those are the rod lengths, not the distances between the free ends.", "3": "The lengths add when the rods are in line; they do not multiply."}'),
  ('PHY-8010', 'Physics', 'Reading a results table', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A student times 10 complete swings of a pendulum for three lengths of string.
Length 0.25 m: 10.0 s
Length 1.00 m: 20.0 s
Length 2.25 m: 30.0 s
If the pattern continues, how long will 10 swings take with a string 4.00 m long?', '["40.0 s", "53.3 s", "35.0 s", "160 s"]', 0, 'The times 10, 20 and 30 s are in the ratio 1 : 2 : 3 while the lengths 0.25, 1.00 and 2.25 m are in the ratio 1 : 4 : 9. So the time depends on the square root of the length. A length of 4.00 m is 16 times the first, and √16 = 4, giving 4 × 10.0 = 40.0 s.', '{"1": "That assumes time is directly proportional to length (30 × 4.00 ÷ 2.25).", "2": "The time does not rise by equal steps for equal steps in length.", "3": "That multiplies by 16 instead of by the square root of 16."}'),
  ('PHY-8011', 'Physics', 'Fair tests', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A student wants to find out whether the mass of a pendulum bob affects the time of one swing. She has bobs of 50 g, 100 g and 200 g.
Which of these must she keep the same in every run for the test to be fair?', '["The mass of the bob", "The time for one swing", "The length of the string", "The material the stopwatch is made of"]', 2, 'Only the thing being tested, the mass, should change. The length of the string also affects the swing time, so it must be held constant or a change in time could not be blamed on the mass alone.', '{"0": "Mass is the variable she is deliberately changing.", "1": "The swing time is what she measures, so it is free to change.", "3": "This has no effect on the result."}'),
  ('PHY-8012', 'Physics', 'Cooling curves', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A beaker of hot water cools in a room at 26 °C. Its temperature is read every 5 minutes.
0 min: 80 °C
5 min: 62 °C
10 min: 50 °C
15 min: 42 °C
Which conclusion do these readings support?', '["The water loses the same number of degrees every 5 minutes", "The water cools more slowly as its temperature gets closer to room temperature", "The water will reach room temperature at 20 minutes", "The water cools faster as its temperature gets closer to room temperature"]', 1, 'The drops are 18 °C, then 12 °C, then 8 °C: each is smaller than the one before. The cooling rate falls as the gap between the water and the room shrinks (54 °C, 36 °C, 24 °C at the start of each interval).', '{"0": "The drops are 18, 12 and 8 °C, which are not equal.", "2": "The next drop will be smaller than 8 °C, so the water will still be well above 26 °C.", "3": "The readings show the opposite: the drops get smaller."}'),
  ('PHY-8013', 'Physics', 'Springs', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Loads are hung on a spring and its extension is measured.
1 N: 2.0 cm
2 N: 4.0 cm
3 N: 6.0 cm
4 N: 8.0 cm
5 N: 11.5 cm
Which statement is supported by these results?', '["The extension is proportional to the load for all five readings", "The extension is proportional to the load only up to 4 N", "The spring needs 2 N to stretch by 1 cm", "The spring breaks at 5 N"]', 1, 'Up to 4 N each extra newton adds 2.0 cm, so extension and load are proportional (0.5 N per cm). At 5 N the extension should be 10.0 cm but is 11.5 cm, so the spring has been stretched past its limit of proportionality somewhere between 4 N and 5 N.', '{"0": "The 5 N reading (11.5 cm) is not on the straight-line pattern (10.0 cm).", "2": "It is the other way round: 1 N stretches it 2 cm.", "3": "It stretched further than expected, but nothing shows it broke."}'),
  ('PHY-8014', 'Physics', 'Floating', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A wooden block has a mass of 400 g and a volume of 500 cm³. It floats in water of density 1.0 g/cm³.
What volume of the block is above the water surface?', '["400 cm³", "100 cm³", "500 cm³", "80 cm³"]', 1, 'A floating object displaces its own weight of liquid, so the block displaces 400 g of water, which is 400 cm³. That much of the block is under water, leaving 500 − 400 = 100 cm³ above the surface.', '{"0": "That is the volume under the water, not above it.", "2": "The block floats, so part of it must be under water.", "3": "0.8 is the fraction submerged; it is not a volume in cm³."}'),
  ('PHY-8015', 'Physics', 'Describing motion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'A car starts from rest and speeds up steadily to 20 m/s in 10 s. It holds 20 m/s for the next 20 s, then slows steadily to rest in 5 s.
How far does it travel altogether?', '["700 m", "550 m", "500 m", "450 m"]', 1, 'While speeding up or slowing steadily the average speed is half of 20 m/s. Distances: 10 m/s × 10 s = 100 m, then 20 m/s × 20 s = 400 m, then 10 m/s × 5 s = 50 m. Total 100 + 400 + 50 = 550 m.', '{"0": "That takes 20 m/s for the whole 35 s, ignoring the time spent speeding up and slowing down.", "2": "That leaves out the 50 m covered while slowing down.", "3": "That leaves out the 100 m covered while speeding up."}'),
  ('PHY-8016', 'Physics', 'Switches', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Lamps L1 and L2 and switch S1 are connected one after another in a single branch. A second branch holds lamp L3 and switch S2. The two branches are connected in parallel across a battery.
S1 is open and S2 is closed. Which lamps light?', '["L1 and L2 only", "L1, L2 and L3", "None of them", "L3 only"]', 3, 'Each branch is joined to the battery independently. The open switch S1 breaks the branch that holds L1 and L2, so neither lights. The L3 branch is complete through the closed switch S2, so L3 lights.', '{"0": "Their branch is broken by the open switch S1.", "1": "The open switch S1 stops the current in the L1 and L2 branch.", "2": "The L3 branch is a complete circuit on its own."}'),
  ('PHY-8017', 'Physics', 'Short circuits', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Two identical lamps, X and Y, are in series with a battery and both glow. A thick copper wire of almost no resistance is then connected directly across the two terminals of lamp X.
What happens?', '["X goes out and Y glows more brightly", "Both lamps go out", "X glows more brightly and Y goes out", "Both lamps stay as they were"]', 0, 'The current takes the easy path through the copper wire instead of through X, so X goes out. The circuit now holds only one lamp''s resistance, so the current is larger and Y, which now has the whole battery voltage across it, glows more brightly.', '{"1": "The circuit is still complete through the wire and lamp Y.", "2": "The wire bypasses X, not Y.", "3": "Removing X''s resistance from the circuit changes the current."}'),
  ('PHY-8018', 'Physics', 'Chain drives', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'On a bicycle the pedals turn a front chainwheel with 48 teeth. A chain links it to a rear sprocket with 16 teeth, which is fixed to the back wheel. The back wheel has a circumference of 2.0 m.
How far does the bicycle move for one full turn of the pedals?', '["2.0 m", "6.0 m", "0.67 m", "96 m"]', 1, 'One pedal turn pulls 48 chain links past the rear sprocket, which turns 48 ÷ 16 = 3 times. The wheel turns with the sprocket, so the bicycle moves 3 × 2.0 = 6.0 m.', '{"0": "That would be true only if both toothed wheels were the same size.", "2": "The small rear sprocket turns more often than the chainwheel, not less.", "3": "That multiplies the tooth count by the circumference."}'),
  ('PHY-8019', 'Physics', 'Belt drives', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'Pulley A has a diameter of 10 cm and pulley B has a diameter of 25 cm. An uncrossed belt runs round both and does not slip. Pulley A turns clockwise at 500 revolutions per minute.
How does pulley B turn?', '["Clockwise at 1250 revolutions per minute", "Anticlockwise at 200 revolutions per minute", "Anticlockwise at 1250 revolutions per minute", "Clockwise at 200 revolutions per minute"]', 3, 'An uncrossed belt makes both pulleys turn the same way. The belt moves at one speed, so rim speeds match: 10 × 500 = 25 × n, giving n = 200 revolutions per minute. The larger pulley turns more slowly.', '{"0": "The larger pulley turns more slowly, not faster.", "1": "Only a crossed belt, or meshed gears, would reverse the direction.", "2": "Wrong direction, and the larger pulley turns more slowly."}'),
  ('PHY-8020', 'Physics', 'Shadows and proportion', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. AKU MBBS admission test, Science and Mathematics Reasoning section (AKU practice resource: Science Reasoning)', 'At a certain time of day a vertical pole 1.5 m tall casts a shadow 2.0 m long on level ground. At the same moment a tower nearby casts a shadow 24 m long.
How tall is the tower?', '["32 m", "18 m", "36 m", "16 m"]', 1, 'The Sun''s rays meet the ground at the same angle for both, so height ÷ shadow is the same: 1.5 ÷ 2.0 = 0.75. The tower is 0.75 × 24 = 18 m tall.', '{"0": "That divides by 0.75 instead of multiplying; the shadows here are longer than the objects.", "2": "That multiplies the shadow by 1.5.", "3": "That does not keep the height-to-shadow ratio of 0.75."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO bank_questions (bank, question_id, subject, chapter)
SELECT 'aku', id, subject, chapter FROM questions WHERE chapter = 'Science reasoning' AND mdcat = 0;
